Author: hkjang <gagagiga@naver.com>
Commit: hkjang <gagagiga@naver.com>

    fix: keep client-supplied props off the server-owned keys on post edit

    Post creation refuses client-supplied provenance: every transport builds a
    postcommand.Command and stripReservedProps drops approval_request_id,
    scheduled_post_id, from_mcp/from_webhook/webhook_depth, override_username,
    override_icon_url, from_me_command, from_plugin/plugin_id and every _moyro_*
    key before the INSERT. Both edit routes -- PUT /api/v4/posts/{postID} and
    PUT /api/v4/posts/{postID}/patch -- handed posts.Update whatever props the
    body carried, so the author of any post could re-introduce on edit exactly
    the keys create had just removed: render their post under another name,
    forge plugin attribution, pass an ordinary post off as a system message
    (_moyro_post_type feeds Post.Type in scanPost), or squat the partial unique
    index on props->>'approval_request_id' that the approval worker dedupes on.
    The same gap ran the other way: a client props map replaced the row's props
    wholesale, erasing the provenance a trusted adapter had stamped.

    postcommand.MergeEditedProps now owns the rule for both routes -- the
    client's props with every reserved key stripped, then the stored row's
    reserved keys re-applied -- so the reserved set keeps one definition and the
    two routes answer the same input the same way. patchPost already reads the
    row it is patching; updatePost now reads it too, reporting only a real
    storage fault as 500 and leaving a missing row to the existing 403.
    pluginhost's UpdatePost sets provenance legitimately and keeps talking to
    posts.Update directly, unchanged.

    Verified against PostgreSQL 16 in an isolated schema with real services,
    handlers and a running ws.Hub: both routes failed the new tests before this
    change (the second route's edit even 500d on the unique-index collision the
    first route's forged approval_request_id created), and reverting either call
    site or the carry-over half fails only that half.
